    "dog": {
      "word": "DOG",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "(Zoöl.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A quadruped of the genus Canis, esp. the domestic dog (C. familiaris). animals for intelligence, docility, and attachment to man. There are numerous carefully bred varieties, as the beagle, bloodhound, bulldog, coachdog, collie, Danish dog, foxhound, greyhound, mastiff, pointer, poodle, St. Bernard, setter, spaniel, spitz dog, terrier, etc. There are also many mixed breeds, and partially domesticated varieties, as well as wild dogs, like the dingo and dhole. (See these names in the Vocabulary.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A mean, worthless fellow; a wretch. What is thy servant, which is but a dog, that he should do this great thing 2 Kings viii. 13 (Rev. Ver. )",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A fellow; -- used humorously or contemptuously; as, a sly dog; a lazy dog. [Colloq.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Astron.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "One of the two constellations, Canis Major and Canis Minor, or the Greater Dog and the Lesser Dog. Canis Major contains the Dog Star (Sirius).",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "An iron for holding wood in a fireplace; a firedog; an andiron.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Mech.) (a) A grappling iron, with a claw or claws, for fastening into wood or other heavy articles, for the purpose of raising or moving them. (b) An iron with fangs fastening a log in a saw pit, or on the carriage of a sawmill. (c) A piece in machinery acting as a catch or clutch; especially, the carrier of a lathe, also, an adjustable stop to change motion, as in a machine tool. sense of relating to, or characteristic of, a dog. It is also used to denote a male; as, dog fox or g-fox, a male fox; dog otter or dog- otter, dog wolf, etc.; -- also to denote a thing of cheap or mean quality; as, dog Latin. A dead dog, a thing of no use or value. 1 Sam. xxiv. 14. from enjoying what would be an advantage to them but is none to him. the Mediterranean region (Thelygonum Cynocrambe). species of flea (Pulex canis) which infests dogs and cats, and is often troublesome to man. In America it is the common flea. See Flea, and Aphaniptera. wheat. earth, rocks, and tree trunks, -- a lobed expansion, dingy green above and whitish with fuscous veins beneath. piliferus; another species is Trichodectes latus. a drum, or on an endless track, as for churning. Asia; -- the gorbuscha; -- called also holia, and hone. Nassidæ, esp. the Nassa reticulata of England. physic to the dogs; I'll none of it.\" Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To hunt or track like a hound; to follow insidiously or indefatigably; to chase with a dog or dogs; to worry, as if by dogs; to hound with importunity. I have been pursued, dogged, and waylaid. Pope. Your sins will dog you, pursue you. Burroughs. Eager ill-bred petitioners, who do not so properly supplicate as hunt the person whom they address to, dogging him from place to place, till they even extort an answer to their rude requests. South.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
